  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Robeson returns three days after his first visit and is seen by Dr. Lindqvist-Adebayo of psychiatry today. His blood tests are normal. He still wakes at three in the morning and cannot get back to sleep, has little appetite, and has spent most of the three days at home. On two of the days he again thought he would be better off dead, without any wish or plan to act; he kept to his safety plan, has put his medicines in a locked box, and has the 988 number in his phone. He has had no alcohol. He asked again who can read his record, and the protections were explained. In the structured interview with Dr. Lindqvist-Adebayo he described a first depression about two years ago, after surgery, which lifted slowly over some months without treatment. Before it he had some weeks of intense, absorbed work, sleeping his usual hours, without elation, irritability, racing thoughts or risky behavior. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Symptoms</td><td>Early waking, poor appetite, low mood and withdrawal unchanged</td></tr><tr><td>Safety</td><td>Passive thoughts of death on two days; no intent or plan; medicines locked</td></tr><tr><td>Results</td><td>Blood count, metabolic panel, calcium, TSH, B12, folate, testosterone and A1c normal; HIV and syphilis tests negative</td></tr><tr><td>Consultation</td><td>Structured interview and bipolar screening with psychiatry today</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

